{{!
	Site footer: the footer landmark and the three rows core builds inside it.

	Rendered once per page by skin.mustache, the only caller, which wraps this partial in
	the footer container element that supplies the surrounding page padding. Each row is
	delegated to Footer__row.mustache, the only partial included here and included three
	times, so the list markup lives in one place and this file owns just the landmark, the
	language attributes and the row order.

	Every name emitted below belongs to MediaWiki core rather than to this skin. The id and
	the class written on the element are part of the platform DOM contract, and each row id
	and portlet class is assigned by SkinComponentMenu, which retains footer ids verbatim
	instead of prefixing them the way it does every other portlet. Gadgets, user styles,
	extensions and the print stylesheet all select on those names, so nothing here is
	renamed and no notion- prefix is introduced anywhere in this file. The Notion restyle
	of the footer lives entirely in resources/skins.notion.styles/components/Footer.less,
	which hangs off exactly these core-owned names, and the suppression of the footer on
	paper lives in resources/skins.notion.styles/layouts/print.less. This partial therefore
	carries no presentational class, no inline style and no print-only markup of its own,
	and it needs no scripting: the footer is fully rendered and fully styled with
	JavaScript disabled.

	One print detail is worth knowing here even though it is not implemented here. Core's
	portlet data does not carry the historic noprint class on the icons row, so the skin
	class appends it to that row's class in the template data, exactly as SkinVector22 does
	and for the reason its own comment gives. It has to arrive through the data: writing it
	into the markup below would hardcode one row's print behaviour into a partial that is
	otherwise driven entirely by what core supplies.

	@typedef object footerItem
	@prop string id of list item element
	@prop string html of list item

	@typedef object footerRow
	@prop string class of list element
	@prop string id of list element
	@prop footerItem[] array-items

	string|null html-user-language-attributes a string of attribute HTML
	footerRow data-portlets.data-footer-info the info row
	footerRow data-portlets.data-footer-places the places row
	footerRow data-portlets.data-footer-icons the icons row

	The three rows are assembled by core's footer component and surfaced as portlets, so
	their ids are footer-info, footer-places and footer-icons and their classes are the
	portlet pair for that id, gaining an extra emptyPortlet class while a row holds no items.
	The skin definition in skin.json additionally lists all three in its menus array, which
	is how a skin declares that it renders those menus itself: core then leaves items that
	extensions contribute through the navigation hook in the portlet data for this template
	to render, instead of folding them back into its own footer component for skins that
	have not opted in. A row that is present but itemless is core reporting an empty
	portlet, not a fault here, and a row missing altogether is a question for that menus
	array and for the skin data set rather than for this file.

	Each row is guarded by a section on its own dotted path, so a data set that omits a row
	emits no list for it at all rather than an empty one. Both halves of each section name
	the identical dotted path on purpose: the compiler treats a mismatched pair as a
	compile error, and because templates are compiled with errors raised as exceptions that
	would be a fatal response on every page rather than a silent gap in the footer. The
	order info, places, icons is the reading order the stylesheet and the copyright
	placement assume, so it is not interchangeable.

	html-user-language-attributes arrives from core already serialised as attribute HTML: a
	dir and lang pair whenever the interface language differs from the content language,
	and an empty string otherwise. It is the one value interpolated in this file, and it is
	emitted with a triple-brace expression because escaping a serialised attribute fragment
	would turn it into visible text in the footer.

	The element below is the native footer landmark. Because it is not nested inside an
	article, aside, main, nav or section element, it maps to the contentinfo role on its own,
	so no role attribute is emitted here and none is needed -- exactly one such landmark is
	emitted per page. Do not add one back: an explicit role would only restate what the
	element already conveys.
}}
<footer id="footer" class="mw-footer" {{{html-user-language-attributes}}}>
	{{#data-portlets.data-footer-info}}{{>Footer__row}}{{/data-portlets.data-footer-info}}
	{{#data-portlets.data-footer-places}}{{>Footer__row}}{{/data-portlets.data-footer-places}}
	{{#data-portlets.data-footer-icons}}{{>Footer__row}}{{/data-portlets.data-footer-icons}}
</footer>
